I'm not sure if it's known but it seems remote dynamic import is quite slow. For example

```
async function main() {
  const t = performance.now();
  await Promise.all([
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
  ]);
  console.log(performance.now() - t);
}
await main();
```

Above took approx. 55 ms with `deno run -A --no-check ./test.ts`. It seems the number of remote imports in the target file affect the performance.

Additionally, it seems remote dynamic import does not use cache. For example

```
// test.ts
async function main() {
  const t = performance.now();
  await Promise.all([
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
    import("<https://deno.land/x/denops_core@v2.0.3/mod.ts>"),
  ]);
  console.log(performance.now() - t);
}
await main();
```

Above took approx. 500 ms with `deno run -A --no-check ./test.ts` even the URL is just repeated.

Say I have this "app":

```
import type {ServerRequest} from "<https://deno.land/std/http/server.ts>";
import { multiParser } from '<https://deno.land/x/multiparser@v2.0.1/mod.ts>';

export default async function(req:ServerRequest) {
	const form = await multiParser(req);
	console.log(form);
}

```

And I have a "runner" that runs such an app dynamically:

```
import type {ServerRequest} from "<https://deno.land/std/http/server.ts>";

async function run() {
	const mod = await import('./app.ts');

	mod.default(&lt;ServerRequest&gt;{});
}

run();

```

Deno tells me I need `--allow-read` for the app.ts and `--allow-net` for the multiparser module. OK.

But now if I do:

```
deno cache app.ts

```

Caching `app.ts` means all the deps are downloaded and future imports of them are served from the local cache. From the CLI help for cache:

```
Future runs of this module will trigger no downloads or compilation unless
--reload is specified.

```

Despite that Deno still requires the same permissions, even though it would not perform a network request at all even if I gave it `--allow-net`.

Given this, can we allow modules that have a remote URL to be used without `--allow-net` if they are present in the cache and therefore trigger no net access?

Thanks :v:

